From 1147b9045e97ee1648beb73b5521d9962c5c5521 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kartheek Palla <kartheek@sanketika.in>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 10:36:17 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] feat(oid4vc-service): refuse another issuer's type, and
 unrequested disclosure
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two holes found in the Education review, both of which let a request succeed
that should not have.

An issuer restricted to its own credentials still minted anybody's. The flag
narrowed the ADVERTISED metadata only, so the credential endpoint went on
resolving the deployment-wide config list: a wallet that named a configuration
id it had learned from another institution got that credential, signed by this
issuer's key. The endpoint now resolves against the same own-authored list the
metadata is built from, and a request for somebody else's type is refused by
name — including a vct-only request, which is the shape a wallet on the
authorization_code path actually sends, and which would otherwise have failed
with the useless "could not determine the credential type".

A holder could also disclose more than was asked for and have it accepted. The
DCQL matcher checked that every requested claim was present and never that no
unrequested one was, so extra disclosures were dropped downstream rather than
refused — a verifier that never asked for a national id could still receive
one over the wire. The matcher now compares the disclosed claim names against
the query and rejects the credential, naming the claims but never their values.
REJECT_UNREQUESTED_DISCLOSURES=false restores the old behaviour for a
deployment that depends on it; unlike the issuance flag this defaults ON,
because accepting undisclosed data is not a safe default.

Fifteen tests, positive and negative for each: an issuer issues its own type
and is refused another's, and a presentation carrying one unrequested claim is
refused where the same presentation without it is accepted.
